    ],
    "proof_tex": "For every deterministic loading,\n\\[\n \\widehat\\tau_g-\\tau_g=\\sum_{i,j}g_{ij}E_{ij}\n\\]\nby the definitions of the score and causal contrast.  Identification makes the summands a row-major scalar martingale-difference sequence.  Their predictable quadratic variation and increment envelope satisfy\n\\[\n \\sum_{i,j}E_P[g_{ij}^2E_{ij}^2\\mid\\mathbb G_{o(i,j)-1}]\n \\le\\frac{2B^2}{q}\\lVert g\\rVert_F^2,\n \\qquad\n \\max_{i,j}|g_{ij}E_{ij}|\n \\le\\frac{3BC_\\infty}{qN}.\n\\]\nThe divisions use \\(q>0\\), and the second inequality uses the dense-loading bound.  The two-sided scalar Freedman inequality implies, with \\(x>0\\),\n\\[\n P\\!\\left\\{|\\widehat\\tau_g-\\tau_g|>\n 2B\\sqrt{\\frac{\\lVert g\\rVert_F^2x}{q}}\n +\\frac{2BC_\\infty x}{qN}\\right\\}\\le2e^{-x}.\n\\]\nTake \\(x=\\log(2d/\\alpha)\\) and apply a union bound over the predeclared family.  The failure probability is at most \\(2de^{-x}=\\alpha\\), proving simultaneous coverage.  The average-loading upper bound \\(\\lVert g\\rVert_F^2\\le C_g/N\\) gives the displayed radius order.  Its leading term is the pointwise martingale scale multiplied by \\(\\sqrt{\\log d}\\); neither the definition nor the proof of \\(b_{nm}\\) contains \\(d\\).",
